/**
\page org_mitk_views_m2_plot M2aia Plot View

\tableofcontents

\section m2plot_overview Overview

The <b>Plot View</b> draws the result of a dimensionality reduction as an interactive point
cloud, in two or three dimensions. A reduction produces two things that belong together but do
not live in the same data: an <b>embedding</b>, one point per pixel, and <b>loadings</b>, one
value per centroid and component. The view plots either of them, so a PCA can be read as the
score plot of its pixels and as the loading plot of its peaks without leaving the view.

A reduction computed over more than one image publishes one embedding per image in the same
component space. Selecting all of them draws them in one cloud, which is what says whether the
images share that space or sit apart in it - a batch effect is a cloud that separates by image
rather than by tissue.

Open it via <em>Window &rarr; Show View &rarr; Spectral Imaging &rarr; Plot</em>.

\section m2plot_input Selecting the input

Five selections feed the view. None of them is mandatory on its own; which ones matter depends
on what is being shown. All but the centroid list take several nodes at once.

- <b>Embedding images</b> - any image with more than one component, such as the results the
  Data Compression view publishes as a child of the source image (PCA, t-SNE, NMF, ICA, PLS-DA),
  or any other multi component result computed for it or read from file. One component is one
  axis of the plot. Selecting several draws them in one cloud; that is what a combined reduction
  is looked at with. Embeddings that do not carry the same number of components are plotted over
  the components they have in common, and a warning is written to the log.
- <b>Masks</b> - a segmentation per embedding. Its group 0 is read, as everywhere else in
  M²aia: the valid spectra of an image, or the clusters of a clustering. It is what colours the
  points by label, which is how one sees whether the classes a segmentation names separate in the
  embedding at all. Labels of different masks that carry the same name share one entry of the
  legend, so the same class of two images is one colour.
- <b>RGB images</b> - an image per embedding whose colour every point takes. This is what a
  three component image is for: read as an RGB image, it puts the colours of the render window
  and the colours of the plot into the same terms. It does not have to be made of bytes - a three
  component image of any type is stretched onto the same range per channel, over all embeddings
  together, so one value is one colour throughout the cloud.
- <b>Intensity images</b> - a single channel image per embedding: an ion image, or one of the
  similarity maps the Embedding Search view computes (cosine, Manhattan, …). It does not colour
  the cloud, it lights it: every point keeps the colour its clustering or its RGB image gives it
  and is dimmed and drained of colour by the value this image holds for its pixel, so the points
  that answer a query stand out of a cloud that still shows what its regions are. The plot follows the images, so a
  map computed while the plot is open recolours the cloud at once.

Each of the three companion selections is matched to the embeddings rather than assigned by hand:
an embedding takes the entry whose grid it shares and, where several of them fit, the one that was
computed from the same image. Describing a combined embedding is therefore a matter of selecting
the mask of every image it was computed from, in any order. Colouring by the RGB image or dimming
by the intensity image is only offered once <em>every</em> embedding has one, because a colouring
half the cloud has no answer for would say more about which images were described than about the
points.
- <b>Centroids with loadings</b> - a centroid list an analysis has attached loadings to. The Data
  Compression view attaches them when <em>Attach component loadings to the peak list</em> is
  checked; they are stored as features named <tt>&lt;image&gt;.&lt;method&gt;.c&lt;component&gt;</tt>.
  Lists without loadings are not offered, because there would be nothing to place their points by.

\section m2plot_points Choosing what is plotted

<b>Show</b> switches between the two clouds:

- <b>Embedding (pixels)</b> - one point per pixel of the embedding images. Pixels that stayed zero
  in every component never took part in the reduction and are left out, so the background does
  not pile up on the origin. <em>Only pixels inside the mask</em> additionally drops every pixel
  the mask leaves unlabelled.
- <b>Loadings (centroids)</b> - one point per centroid of the peak list, placed by its loadings.
  A centroid list can carry the loadings of several runs and methods side by side; the
  <b>Analysis</b> box picks the one that is drawn.

<b>X axis</b>, <b>Y axis</b> and <b>Z axis</b> put any component on any axis. Leaving the Z axis
at <em>&mdash;</em> draws the cloud in the plane, with a parallel projection and the camera
fixed, so it behaves like an ordinary scatter plot; choosing a third component turns it into a
cloud in space that the left mouse button orbits.

<b>Max. points</b> caps how many points are drawn. When the data offers more, an evenly spread
sample of that size is taken, always the same one for the same data, so a large dataset stays
interactive. The cap is shared out over all selected embeddings in proportion to what each of them
offers, so no image crowds the others out. The line under the controls says how many points are
drawn, from how many images, and how many were available.

\section m2plot_appearance Colours and interaction

<b>Colour by</b> decides what the colour of a point means:

- <b>Image</b> - which of the selected embeddings the point came from, in the colour and under
  the name of its node. Only offered while more than one is drawn, and then the colouring the view
  starts with: whether the clouds of two images lie on top of each other or beside each other is
  the first thing to read off a combined embedding.
- <b>RGB image</b> - the colour the RGB image of its embedding gives the pixel, taken as it is.
  Only offered while every embedding has a fitting image.
- <b>Pixel position (RGB)</b> - where the pixel lies in the image, with the three axes on the
  three colour channels. Reading it against the shape of the cloud shows how much of the
  embedding is spatial: neighbouring pixels landing next to each other means the reduction has
  kept the tissue together, while an even wash of colour means it has not. An axis of no extent -
  the third one of a single slice - stays dark.
- <b>Mask label</b> - the label the mask of its embedding gives the pixel, drawn in the colour and
  under the name the segmentation itself carries. Only offered while at least one fitting mask is
  selected; pixels of an embedding without one are drawn as unlabelled.
- <b>m/z</b> - the position of the centroid, in the loading plot.
- <b>Component n</b> - the value of that component, on a continuous ramp. Putting a component on
  the colour rather than on an axis is how a fourth dimension is read off a three dimensional
  plot.
- <b>Nothing</b> - one colour for every point.

<b>Brightness from intensity image</b> switches the highlighting on and off. A point it does not
answer for loses its light <em>and</em> its colour: it is both darkened and drained towards grey,
keeping about a third of its chroma, so the points a query does answer for are the only saturated
colours in the plot while the cloud around them still shows where its regions are.

Two numbers say how hard that is applied. <b>Emphasis</b> bends the ramp: at one a point is dimmed
in proportion to its value, and the higher it goes the closer to the top of the range a point has
to be to stay bright. <b>Darkest</b> says how much a point of the lowest intensity keeps: zero
fades it into the background, one leaves every point at full brightness and switches the effect
off.

The ramp is stretched over the 2nd to the 98th percentile of the values rather than over their
extremes, and anything beyond is clamped. This is what makes the highlighting visible on maps that
are not spread evenly: the values of a cosine similarity sit between 0.8 and 1.0, so a single
outlying pixel used to compress every real difference into the top tenth of the scale and the
cloud came out uniformly bright. The range runs over all embeddings together, so what the maps of
two images say stays comparable. The scalar bar keeps naming what the colours mean while they are
dimmed.

The mouse orbits (three dimensions only), pans and zooms the cloud; <b>Reset view</b> frames it
again. A click that is not a drag picks the point under the cursor and describes it below the
controls: a pixel by its image, its index and its label, a centroid by its m/z and its
annotation, both together with their coordinates on the current axes.

\section m2plot_notes Notes

- A mask or an RGB image whose grid differs from the embedding it would describe is ignored
  rather than used; colouring points by whatever happens to lie at the same offset in another
  image would be misleading. A warning is written to the log in that case, naming the embedding
  that was left without one.
- Changing an axis, the colour, the brightness or the point size does not read the image again;
  only changing a selection, the mask restriction or the point cap does. The intensity image is
  the exception: it is read again whenever it changes, which is what lets a similarity map appear
  in the plot as soon as the Embedding Search view has computed it.

*/
